e sentence. Do not output any additional explanation. Only output the summary.\nSentence: China's next export boom may involve college-educated workers."}], [{'role': 'user', 'content': 'Summarize the input sentence while preserving the exact meaning of the sentence. Do not output any additional explanation. Only output the summary.\nSentence: The Livestrong brand has survived beyond Lance Armstrong.'}], [{'role': 'user', 'content': 'Summarize the input sentence while preserving the exact meaning of the sentence. Do not output any additional explanation. Only output the summary.\nSentence: The Livestrong brand has a future beyond Lance Armstrong.'}], [{'role': 'user', 'content': 'Summarize the input sentence while preserving the exact meaning of the sentence. Do not output any additional explanation. Only output the summary.\nSentence: After a tumultuous chapter, Livestrong continues to thrive.'}], [{'role': 'user', 'content': 'Summarize the input sentence while preserving the exact meaning of the sentence. Do not output any additional explanation. Only output the summary.\nSentence: The Livestrong brand endures despite the departure of its founder, Lance Armstrong.'}], [{'role': 'user', 'content': 'Summarize the input sentence while preserving the exact meaning of the sentence. Do not output any additional explanation. Only output the summary.\nSentence: The brand continues after its founder.'}], [{'role': 'user', 'content': 'Summarize the input sentence while preserving the exact meaning of the sentence. Do not output any additional explanation. Only output the summary.\nSentence: The brand has life after its founder.'}], [{'role': 'user', 'content': 'Summarize the input sentence while preserving the exact meaning of the sentence. Do not output any additional explanation. Only output the summary.\nSentence: The Livestrong brand endures beyond Lance Armstrong.'}], [{'role': 'user', 'content': 'Summarize the input sentence while preserving the exact meaning of the sentence. Do not output any additional explanation. Only output the summary.\nSentence: LIVESTRONG continues beyond Lance Armstrong.'}], [{'role': 'user', 'content': 'Summarize the input sentence while preserving the exact meaning of the sentence. Do not output any additional explanation. Only output the summary.\nSentence: Frequently Asked Questions About the Flu.'}], [{'role': 'user', 'content': 'Summarize the input sentence while preserving the exact meaning of the sentence. Do not output any additional explanation. Only output the summary.\nSentence: Frequently asked questions about the flu answered.'}], [{'role': 'user', 'content': 'Summarize the input sentence while preserving the exact meaning of the sentence. Do not output any additional explanation. Only output the summary.\nSentence: 1. What are the symptoms of the flu?\n \n2. How is the flu spread?\n \n3. What are the different types of the flu?\n \n4. Can the flu be treated with antibiotics?\n \n5. What is the difference between a cold and the flu?\n \n6. Who is most at risk for developing complications from the flu?\n \n7. How can I prevent the flu?\n \n8. What is the flu vaccine and how effective is it?\n \n9. How long is someone contagious with the flu?\n \n10. What are the possible complications of the flu?'}], [{'role': 'user', 'content': 'Summarize the input sentence while preserving the exact meaning of the sentence. Do not output any additional explanation. Only output the summary.\nSentence: 1. What is the flu?\n \nThe flu, also known as influenza, is a contagious respiratory illness caused by the influenza virus. It can cause mild to severe illness and can lead to complications like pneumonia, bronchitis, and sinus and ear infections.\n\n2. What are the symptoms of the flu?\n\nThe symptoms of the flu include fever, cough, sore throat, runny or stuffy nose, body aches, headaches, and fatigue. Some people may also experience diarrhea and vomiting, especially children.\n\n3. How is the flu spread?\n\nThe flu is spread through the air when an infected person coughs or sneezes, releasing droplets that contain the virus. People can also get the flu by touching a surface that has the virus on it and then touching their mouth, nose, or eyes.\n\n4. How long is the flu contagious?\n\nPeople with the flu are usually contagious for 5 to 7 days, but some people may be contagious for up to 2 weeks.\n\n5. Can the flu be treated?\n\nYes, the flu can be treated with antiviral medications like oseltamivir (Tamiflu) and zanamivir (Relenza). These medications can help shorten the duration and severity of the flu.\n\n6. What are the complications of the flu?\n\nComplications of the flu include pneumonia, bronchitis, sinus and ear infections, and worsening of chronic health conditions like heart disease, diabetes, and lung disease.\n\n7. Who is at high risk for complications from the flu?\n\nPeople at high risk for complications from the flu include:\n- Older adults (65 years and older)\n- Young children (under 5 years)\n- Pregnant women\n- People with certain chronic health conditions like heart disease, diabetes, and lung disease\n- People with weakened immune systems\n\n8. How can I prevent the flu?\n\nYou can prevent the flu by getting a flu vaccine each year, washing your hands frequently, avoiding close contact with people who are sick, and avoiding touching your mouth, nose, and eyes.\n\n9. Can I get the flu from the flu vaccine?\n\nNo, the flu vaccine cannot give you the flu. However, you may experience side effects like soreness, redness, or swelling where the shot was given, as well as fever, headache, or muscle aches.\n\n10. When should I seek medical attention if I have the flu?\n\nYou should seek medical attention if you experience:\n- Difficulty breathing or shortness of breath\n- Chest pain or pressure\n- Sudden dizziness or fainting\n- Fever above 103°F (39.4°C)\n- Severe headache or confusion\n- Vomiting that lasts more than 2 days\n- Diarrhea that lasts more than 2 days'}], [{'role': 'user', 'content': 'Summarize the input sentence while preserving the exact meaning of the sentence. Do not output any additional explanation. Only output the summary.\nSentence: Here are the top 10 flu questions answered:\n\n1. **What is the flu?**\nThe flu, also known as influenza, is a contagious respiratory illness caused by the influenza virus. It can cause mild to severe illness and can lead to complications such as pneumonia and bronchitis.\n\n2. **What are the symptoms of the flu?**\nThe symptoms of the flu can include fever, cough, sore throat, runny or stuffy nose, body aches, headaches, and fatigue. Some people may also experience diarrhea and vomiting, especially children.\n\n3. **How is the flu spread?**\nThe flu is spread through the air when an infected person coughs or sneezes, releasing droplets that contain the virus. It can also be spread through close contact with an infected person, such as shaking hands or touching surfaces that have the virus on them.\n\n4. **Who is most at risk for the flu?**\nCertain groups of people are at higher risk for complications from the flu, including:\n\t* Older adults (65+ years)\n\t* Young children (under 5 years)\n\t* Pregnant women\n\t* People with certain chronic health conditions, such as heart disease, diabetes, or lung disease\n\t* People with weakened immune systems, such as those with HIV/AIDS or taking immunosuppressive medications\n\n5. **How can I prevent the flu?**\nThere are several ways to prevent the flu, including:\n\t* Getting a flu vaccine each year\n\t* Practicing good hygiene, such as washing your hands frequently and avoiding close contact with people who are sick\n\t* Avoiding touching your eyes, nose, and mouth\n\t* Staying home from work or school if you are sick\n\n6. **What is the flu vaccine?**\nThe flu vaccine is a vaccine that is designed to protect against the flu virus. It is available in two forms: the flu shot and the nasal spray vaccine. The flu shot is an inactivated vaccine that is given by injection, while the nasal spray vaccine is a live, attenuated vaccine that is sprayed into the nostrils.\n\n7. **How effective is the flu vaccine?**\nThe flu vaccine is not 100% effective, but it can still provide significant protection against the flu. The effectiveness of the flu vaccine can vary from year to year, depending on the match between the vaccine and the circulating strains of the flu virus.\n\n8. **What are the complications of the flu?**\nThe flu can lead to several complications, including:\n\t* Pneumonia\n\t* Bronchitis\n\t* Sinus and ear infections\n\t* Worsening of underlying chronic health conditions\n\t* Respiratory failure\n\t* Cardiac arrest\n\n9. **How is the flu diagnosed?**\nThe flu is typically diagnosed based on a combination of symptoms and a physical exam. A healthcare provider may also order laboratory tests, such as a rapid flu test or a PCR test, to confirm the diagnosis.\n\n10. **What is the treatment for the flu?**\nThere is no cure for the flu, but there are several treatments that can help alleviate symptoms and prevent complications. These include:\n\t* Antiviral medications, such as oseltamivir (Tamiflu) and zanamivir (Relenza)\n\t* Pain relievers, such as acetaminophen (Tylenol) and ibuprofen (Advil, Motrin)\n\t* Rest and hydration\n\t* Over-the-counter medications, such as decongestants and cough suppressants'}], [{'role': 'user', 'content': "Summarize the input sentence while preserving the exact meaning of the sentence. Do not output any additional explanation. Only output the summary.\nSentence: Here are the answers to your top 10 flu questions:\n\n1. **What is the flu?**\nThe flu, also known as influenza, is a contagious respiratory illness caused by the influenza virus. It can lead to mild to severe illness and can be life-threatening in some cases.\n\n2. **What are the symptoms of the flu?**\nCommon symptoms of the flu include:\n- Fever\n- Cough\n- Sore throat\n- Runny or stuffy nose\n- Headache\n- Fatigue\n- Muscle or body aches\n- Chills\n- Diarrhea and vomiting (more common in children than adults)\n\n3. **How is the flu spread?**\nThe flu is spread through the air when an infected person coughs, sneezes, or talks. It can also be spread by touching contaminated surfaces and then touching one's mouth, nose, or eyes.\n\n4. **Who is most at risk for the flu?**\nCertain groups are at higher risk for developing serious flu complications, including:\n- Older adults (65+ years)\n- Young children (under 5 years)\n- Pregnant women\n- People with certain chronic health conditions (e.g., heart disease, lung disease, diabetes)\n- People with weakened immune systems (e.g., HIV/AIDS, cancer)\n\n5. **How can I prevent the flu?**\nTo prevent the flu, follow these steps:\n- Get vaccinated against the flu each year\n- Practice good hygiene (e.g., wash hands frequently, avoid touching eyes, nose, and mouth)\n- Avoid close contact with people who are sick\n- Stay home from work or school if you're sick\n\n6. **What is the flu vaccine?**\nThe flu vaccine is an annual vaccine that protects against the most common flu viruses. It's available in various forms, including injectable and nasal spray vaccines.\n\n7. **How effective is the flu vaccine?**\nThe flu vaccine is effective in preventing the flu, but its effectiveness can vary from year to year. On average, the vaccine is 40-60% effective in preventing the flu.\n\n8. **Can I get the flu from the flu vaccine?**\nNo, you cannot get the flu from the flu vaccine. However, you may experience side effects, such as soreness, redness, or swelling at the injection site, or mild flu-like symptoms (e.g., fever, headache).\n\n9. **How long is the flu contagious?**\nPeople with the flu are contagious from 1-2 days before symptoms appear to 5-7 days after becoming sick. Children and people with weakened immune systems may be contagious for a longer period.\n\n10. **What should I do if I get the flu?**\nIf you get the flu, follow these steps:\n- Stay home from work or school to avoid spreading the flu to others\n- Rest and stay hydrated\n- Use over-the-counter medications to relieve symptoms (e.g., pain relievers, decongestants)\n- Consider antiviral medications (e.g., oseltamivir, zanamivir) to help shorten the duration and severity of the flu\n- Consult a healthcare professional if you experience severe symptoms or are at high risk for complications."}], [{'role': 'user', 'content': 'Summarize the input sentence while preserving the exact meaning of the sentence. Do not output any additional explanation. Only output the summary.\nSentence: Common flu questions explained.'}], [{'role': 'user', 'content': 'Summarize the input sentence while preserving the exact meaning of the sentence. Do not output any additional explanation. Only output the summary.\nSentence: Common flu questions explained.'}], [{'role': 'user', 'content': 'Summarize the input sentence while preserving the exact meaning of the sentence. Do not output any additional explanation. Only output the summary.\nSentence: The manufacturer has acknowledged a 40% failure rate in their hip implants.'}], [{'role': 'user', 'content': 'Summarize the input sentence while preserving the exact meaning of the sentence. Do not output any additional explanation. Only output the summary.\nSentence: Manufacturer informed of 40 percent hip implant failure rate.'}], [{'role': 'user', 'content': 'Summarize the input sentence while preserving the exact meaning of the sentence. Do not output any additional explanation. Only output the summary.\nSentence: A maker is alerted to a 40 percent failure rate in hip implants.'}], [{'role': 'user', 'content': 'Summarize the input sentence while preserving the exact meaning of the sentence. Do not output any additional explanation. Only output the summary.\nSentence: A manufacturer has become aware of a 40% failure rate in hip implants.'}], [{'role': 'user', 'content': 'Summarize the input sentence w